/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A solenoid is a coil wound on a tube with a soft-iron plunger inside.
   - The coil is a resistor with inductance.
   - P is coil heating in watts; I is coil current in amperes; R is coil resistance in ohms; ton is the on time in seconds; T is the total cycle time in seconds.
   - A relay is a solenoid plus a set of contacts.
   - A relay has two independent ratings.
   - Contacts come as normally open (NO, closed only when the coil is energised) and normally closed (NC, closed when the coil is off).
   - A DC motor has a magnetic field (from a winding or from permanent magnets) and an armature carrying current.
   - Eb is back emf in volts; V is supply voltage in volts; Ia is armature current in amperes; Ra is armature resistance in ohms; T is torque in newton metres; φ is flux per pole in webers; k is a motor constant.
   - Back emf is also proportional to speed, Eb = kφω with ω in rad/s, so speed ≈ (V − IaRa) ÷ (kφ).
   - A three-phase induction motor has three stator windings fed 120° apart.
*/
