/* book Worked example 2: Q: A 24 V solenoid valve has a coil resistance of 12 Ω. Its driver applies 24 V to pull in, then drops to 6 V to hold. The valve is energised for 2 s in every 20 s. Find (a) the pull-in current and power, (b) the hold current and power, and (c) the duty cycle and the average power if it were held at 24 V.
   1. (a) Pull-in: I = V/R = 24 / 12 = 2.0 A; P = V I = 24 × 2.0 = 48 W.
   2. (b) Hold: I = 6 / 12 = 0.5 A; P = I²R = 0.5² × 12 = 3.0 W, one sixteenth of the pull-in power, because power goes as V².
   3. (c) Duty cycle = ton / T × 100 % = 2 / 20 × 100 % = 10 %. Held at 24 V the average power would be 0.10 × 48 = 4.8 W.
   4. Check: with the peak-and-hold driver the coil runs at 3.0 W while on, far below 48 W, so the same coil could be held continuously without overheating.
   Answer: Pull-in draws 2.0 A and 48 W, hold 0.5 A and 3.0 W; the duty cycle is 10 %, an average of 4.8 W without the hold stage. */
