/* book: lead: A stepper motor turns in fixed angular steps. Each pulse from the driver advances the shaft by one step, and the controller counts pulses to know position without feedback. A servo motor closes a loop: an encoder or potentiometer reports the shaft angle, the drive compares it with the command, and the motor corrects the error. Steppers suit cheap positioning in printers, 3D printers and small CNC  */
/* book Figure 6.6.1: Left: in full-step drive one stator phase is on at a time, and the rotor lines up with it. Right: the 1.8° motor turns a 5 mm lead screw, so 1000 pulses/s give 5 rev/s (300 rpm) and 25 mm/s, and each pulse moves the table 0.025 mm. Phases are drawn simplified; a real 1.8° motor has many rotor teeth. */
