/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A stepper motor turns in discrete angular steps.
   - θs is the step angle in degrees; N is the number of full steps per revolution.
   - Three common constructions exist.
   - Full-step drive energises one or two phases at a time.
   - Pull-in torque is the largest load torque at which the motor can start and run without losing steps at a given pulse rate.
   - Servo motor means a motor used inside a closed loop.
   - Feedback is a potentiometer for a cheap angle measurement, or an encoder for digital position counts.
   - DC servo uses a permanent-magnet DC motor with an encoder and a PWM drive.
   - A lead screw converts rotation into straight motion.
   - v is table speed in mm/s; f is pulse frequency in pulses per second; p is the lead in mm/rev; N is steps per revolution.
*/
