/* book Worked example 2: Q: A syringe pump moves the plunger of a 50 mL syringe (inner diameter 26.7 mm) with a 1.8° stepper at 1/16 microstepping on a lead screw of 1 mm lead. Find (a) microsteps per revolution and plunger travel per microstep, (b) volume per microstep, and (c) the pulse rate for an infusion of 10 mL/h.
   1. (a) Full steps: 360 / 1.8 = 200 per revolution; microsteps: 200 × 16 = 3200 per revolution. Travel per microstep = 1 mm / 3200 = 3.125 × 10⁻⁴ mm = 0.3125 µm.
   2. (b) Plunger area: A = π (26.7/2)² = π × 13.35² = 559.9 mm². Volume per microstep = 559.9 × 3.125 × 10⁻⁴ = 0.175 mm³ = 0.175 µL.
   3. (c) 10 mL/h = 10 000 mm³/h, so plunger speed = 10 000 / 559.9 = 17.86 mm/h = 4.961 × 10⁻³ mm/s.
   4. Pulse rate = speed × microsteps per mm = 4.961 × 10⁻³ × 3200 = 15.9 pulses per second.
   5. Check: 15.9 pulses/s × 0.175 µL × 3600 s = 10 000 µL = 10 mL in one hour, as set.
   Answer: 3200 microsteps per revolution, 0.3125 µm and 0.175 µL per microstep; 10 mL/h needs about 15.9 pulses per second. */
