/* book exam traps: Step angle is not the final resolution after microstepping. A 1.8° motor with 1/16 microstepping gives 0.1125° per commanded microstep, but the mechanical step is still 1.8° and small moves have low incremental torque. | Pull-in torque and pull-out torque are not the same. Pull-in is the limit for starting and running; pull-out is the limit while already running. Pull-in is lower. | Pulse frequency does not multiply speed by steps per revolution. Shaft speed is f/N rev/s, so more steps per revolution means slower speed for the same pulse rate. | Pitch and lead are equal only for a single-start screw; use the lead (travel per revolution), and use the steps per revolution of the drive mode actually set. | A servo drive with the feedback sign reversed drives the error larger, not smaller. The shaft runs away instead of holding position. */
